The Defence Forces have participated in UN peacekeeping and other operations since the 1950s. The UN’s use of the Irish Defence Forces is based on the UN’s need for experienced, well-trained and disciplined personnel, and the Defence Forces’ ability to provide this at short notice. The Irish Defence Forces are currently deployed in the following six missions:
United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS);
United Nations Disengagement Observer Force (UNDOF);
United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS);
United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in the Central African Republic (MINUSCA);
United Nations Interim Security Force for Abyei (UNISFA); and
United Nations Mission in the Republic of South Sudan (UNMISS).
The Defence Forces have been deployed in the UN’s peacekeeping missions for the past 60 years and have provided significant contributions to the UN’s peacekeeping efforts over that period. The Defence Forces currently participate in UN peacekeeping operations in three locations in Africa. The Defence Forces are deployed in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), South Sudan and the Central African Republic. In Africa, the Defence Forces have been deployed in the DRC, South Sudan, the Central African Republic, Sierra Leone, Somalia, Sudan and Liberia.
The Defence Forces are committed to supporting the UN’s peacekeeping operations. It is not a question of whether or not the Defence Forces will be deployed in future, but where and when they will be deployed. As the Chief of Staff indicated in a recent interview, the Defence Forces are a professional organisation which has the flexibility and experience to respond to the UN’s needs when requested.
